484
00:34:24,420 --> 00:34:29,679
Speaker 1: So blogging, because we don't want to talk about Blue Sky specifically, but we've never really talked about blogging.

485
00:34:30,023 --> 00:34:31,940
Speaker 0: Blue Sky, I guess they call it microblogging.

486
00:34:32,221 --> 00:34:32,840
Speaker 1: It's not even.

487
00:34:33,800 --> 00:34:39,000
Speaker 1: Microblogging is like microblog, where I can post small, tiny articles that get syndicated.

488
00:34:39,761 --> 00:34:43,040
Speaker 1: I would characterize Twitter and Blue Sky and things as just social media.

489
00:34:45,100 --> 00:34:48,080
Speaker 0: But basically, back in the day, people had websites.

490
00:34:48,480 --> 00:34:56,293
Speaker 0: And you would structure your website, usually if you're normal, just an individual with a personal site or a fan site, you would just have pages, right?

491
00:34:58,363 --> 00:35:00,940
Speaker 0: And you would just have some sort of navigation and organization.

492
00:35:01,722 --> 00:35:04,838
Speaker 0: On my website, I was like, "Here's video game

493
00:35:04,879 --> 00:35:05,140
Speaker 0: section."

494
00:35:05,140 --> 00:35:06,659
Speaker 0: I had a page per video game.

495
00:35:07,180 --> 00:35:09,820
Speaker 0: And then it was alphabetical order, I guess.

496
00:35:10,521 --> 00:35:18,660
Speaker 0: Even if you couldn't tell what was new, unless I edited my GeoCities HTML to put the new ones at top with a new flag on them.

497
00:35:18,781 --> 00:35:20,259
Speaker 1: I ran three websites.

498
00:35:20,681 --> 00:35:36,520
Speaker 1: I ran Mephistopheles Realm, which was an alphabetical order, a list of NES and SNES games, and links to download translations of the Japanese ROMs that I dispersed across a bunch of GeoCities sites.

499
00:35:37,561 --> 00:35:40,420
Speaker 1: One of them was the site for a clan in an MMO called The Realm.

500
00:35:40,780 --> 00:35:53,700
Speaker 1: But the third one was a blog I ran, but by blog, what I did is, whenever I typed something, I would edit HTML raw, and I would just put my new post at the top of a giant table.

501
00:35:56,820 --> 00:35:59,194
Speaker 0: And so blogging wasn't really a thing.

502
00:36:00,461 --> 00:36:04,780
Speaker 0: The idea of blogging was, it's just, when I say something, I post it on a website.

503
00:36:05,402 --> 00:36:08,420
Speaker 0: And that is much older than the idea of blogging, right?

504
00:36:08,840 --> 00:36:20,640
Speaker 0: So it's like, when did blogging itself start, versus when did the word blog and people blogging intentionally with that structure and mindset come in, right?

505
00:36:20,780 --> 00:36:25,200
Speaker 0: Because people were doing it without labeling it or thinking about it as such.

506
00:36:26,064 --> 00:36:28,900
Speaker 0: They were just writing thoughts and putting them on websites.

507
00:36:29,082 --> 00:36:29,518
Speaker 0: An example.

508
00:36:30,060 --> 00:36:35,400
Speaker 1: Scott recorded audio of him saying words about things, put it on the internet.

509
00:36:35,762 --> 00:36:36,894
Speaker 1: Was that a podcast?

510
00:36:37,157 --> 00:36:37,319
Speaker 1: No.

511
00:36:38,440 --> 00:36:39,716
Speaker 0: I mean, it was in an RSS feed.

512
00:36:40,981 --> 00:36:42,659
Speaker 0: Podcasts did exist at that point.

513
00:36:42,861 --> 00:36:43,899
Speaker 1: Yep, it might have been.

514
00:36:43,940 --> 00:36:46,439
Speaker 0: But I didn't know the word podcast when I did it.

515
00:36:46,820 --> 00:36:46,913
Speaker 0: Yep.

516
00:36:47,441 --> 00:36:51,940
Speaker 1: I remember when we launched Geek Nights, you basically said, "Hey, there's this thing called podcasting.

517
00:36:51,960 --> 00:36:52,515
Speaker 1: We should do

518
00:36:52,535 --> 00:36:52,576
Speaker 1: it.".

519
00:36:52,882 --> 00:36:57,400
Speaker 0: At that point, I had heard the word, but I didn't really understand it until iPod Nano came out.

520
00:36:58,140 --> 00:37:11,400
Speaker 0: So anyway, the concept of blog was like, okay, I will intentionally post in chronological order, right, my thoughts in sort of long form text to the website on a regular basis.

521
00:37:11,984 --> 00:37:14,540
Speaker 0: And then eventually RSS came around.

522
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:22,360
Speaker 0: You know, the word blog, it's like someone coined the word blog based on web blog, right, just removing the "weh" part.

523
00:37:23,580 --> 00:37:32,974
Speaker 0: But then there was, you know, software blogger, right, really caused that word to be the word you used for, "I am posting to this

524
00:37:33,378 --> 00:37:33,580
Speaker 0: website.".

525
00:37:33,621 --> 00:37:45,259
Speaker 1: Well, because before that point, like even if what I ran on GeoCities in the '90s was technically by modern standards a blog, I was doing it manually and I had to know HTML and I literally just wrote HTML.

526
00:37:47,183 --> 00:37:57,820
Speaker 1: Once the technology was sort of separated from the content creation, I think that was the genesis of blogging, because then someone could have a blog.

527
00:37:58,162 --> 00:38:00,836
Speaker 1: who is not an expert in making a website work?

528
00:38:00,876 --> 00:38:01,077
Speaker 1: in the '90s.

529
00:38:02,062 --> 00:38:26,407
Speaker 0: Before the idea of blogging, even though some websites were blogs, most people were using a Usenet or an email list or a BBS or something of that nature to do blogging-type activities, because in those places, people could follow, right, with an email list, you would get the emails of the new posts versus where.

530
00:38:26,427 --> 00:38:32,519
Speaker 0: if you had a website, people just kind of, it was like early web comics, people just sort of check your website every day and see if there was a new comic.

531
00:38:32,700 --> 00:38:38,700
Speaker 1: Yep, I used to open all my bookmarks of Penny Arcade and MegaTokyo in real life to see if they had an update.

532
00:38:39,300 --> 00:39:00,160
Speaker 0: Right, so even though blogging originated in the later '90s, it didn't really explode until the RSS came around in the earlier 2000s, right, and people were actually able to follow blogs and see new posts coming in and check on them in sort of a feed-like manner.

533
00:39:01,465 --> 00:39:13,899
Speaker 0: And when that happened, right, you know, there was no Twitters or anything, no social networks really, you know, they were very early, you know, Facebook was like still college-only kind of thing.

534
00:39:14,621 --> 00:39:15,519
Speaker 1: Yep, Orchid, we used Orchid.

535
00:39:16,342 --> 00:39:19,212
Speaker 0: Yeah, but that wasn't a place that you could really blog.

536
00:39:19,495 --> 00:39:20,220
Speaker 0: very well, right?

537
00:39:20,300 --> 00:39:27,810
Speaker 1: I ran a mailing list called Rims Rants between 1999 and late 2000 that I then stopped.

538
00:39:30,585 --> 00:39:32,160
Speaker 1: That I also cross-posted to Usenet.

539
00:39:34,480 --> 00:39:56,159
Speaker 0: Yeah, and so, right, once the, you know, people got RSS, people started blogging a lot 'cause you can make money on it, get followers, right, become popular, go on to be, you know, get noticed, become a real journalist or whatever it is, you know, if you were writing about something well enough, whereas if you did that on a website earlier than that, nothing would come of it 'cause no one would see you.

540
00:39:57,420 --> 00:40:05,483
Speaker 0: Then the social networks came along and they actually sapped a lot of the energy out of blogging because I, you know, at least for me personally, it's like you.

541
00:40:05,523 --> 00:40:22,940
Speaker 0: look at the blogs I've had over the years, I started in, I guess I had the website that wasn't a blog technically, then in college I used the software Blossom, which is like a Perl script basically, a CGI Perl script, where you would put text files in a folder and that would cause them to be posts on the blog.

542
00:40:23,462 --> 00:40:28,020
Speaker 0: and then that's also where I put that not a podcast thing, I was in the same platform.

543
00:40:29,601 --> 00:40:29,847
Speaker 0: Self-hosted.

544
00:40:31,021 --> 00:40:56,995
Speaker 0: And, you know, I wrote stuff there regularly and then I switched to WordPress, I guess around when we were in Beacon Times and then once there was like the social networks really picked up and I was Twittering, my energy of writing and like what I had something to say and I just wanted to say it, I didn't care if, you know, if anyone read it really, but I had the things to put out into the world and I put them out into the world on, you know, Twitters or whatever I was using at

545
00:40:57,015 --> 00:40:57,439
Speaker 1: the time.

546
00:40:57,681 --> 00:41:01,520
Speaker 1: Like sometimes I'd post in our own forum if it was really long and then link to that on Twitter.

547
00:41:02,342 --> 00:41:25,019
Speaker 0: Yeah, but it's like my energy for writing on the blog was going, you know, it was elsewhere, so I didn't really blog a lot, you know, even though I had, like I think my word, like I keep these websites up just for even though I wrote a bunch of really bad shit I don't agree with probably still on there and someone could probably go digging and make sure I never win any political campaign ever, but I'm not gonna try anyway, so it doesn't matter.

548
00:41:29,302 --> 00:41:34,020
Speaker 0: Oh yeah, I keep the blogs up, but I stop blogging, right?

549
00:41:34,241 --> 00:41:42,577
Speaker 0: Because my writing energy was going elsewhere and now that I'm not using the social media, I got the energy again, but now I have to go make a website again.

550
00:41:43,944 --> 00:41:51,179
Speaker 1: Yeah, that's why I took the interim step of setting up micro.blog because it just handles that and I have access to the database, but I will eventually.

551
00:41:51,260 --> 00:41:55,380
Speaker 0: It's better if I say, if I do that, all the energy will be sapped out again.

552
00:41:55,720 --> 00:41:56,096
Speaker 1: I can't do that.

553
00:41:56,320 --> 00:42:06,285
Speaker 1: Well, I'm letting my energy go 'cause this is sort of the fundamental thing and this is, so unrelatedly, I'm doing a lot of research into modernity, partly because there's a panel Emily and I are doing at MagFest.

554
00:42:06,568 --> 00:42:08,000
Speaker 1: that is not a Raymond Scott panel.

555
00:42:08,080 --> 00:42:38,820
Speaker 1: that's on post-postmodernism and I'm doing actual research, but disentangling how you create something from creating something is a big fucking deal because that allows people who have, for example, Scott and I, we get our ideas out about a lot of things, not just computers, but video games and board games and politics and stuff, but we have more voice than a lot of humans because one, we have money, that's a separate problem, but two, we know technology.

556
00:42:39,101 --> 00:42:40,440
Speaker 1: We're running this infrastructure.

557
00:42:41,041 --> 00:42:43,700
Speaker 1: You're watching our livestream because I got a bunch of shit set up everywhere.

558
00:42:44,080 --> 00:42:45,997
Speaker 1: Scott made a website to syndicate it.

559
00:42:48,364 --> 00:43:03,240
Speaker 1: There are a lot of people who are very smart, way smarter than us, who know a lot about things that are not technology related, but if they need to know as much as we know about technology to get those ideas out there, unless they're really lucky, those ideas will go nowhere.

560
00:43:03,962 --> 00:43:12,460
Speaker 1: As soon as you can let the people who are experts in things get their ideas out there without them needing to become experts in additional things, that transforms the world.

561
00:43:12,744 --> 00:43:13,759
Speaker 1: That's a big deal.

562
00:43:14,560 --> 00:43:16,059
Speaker 0: - And so that's what happened with blogging.

563
00:43:16,140 --> 00:43:22,940
Speaker 0: is that once we had that sort of structure in mind, people built platforms like Blogger, but also WordPress.

564
00:43:23,880 --> 00:43:24,874
Speaker 1: - Tumblr, arguably.

565
00:43:26,300 --> 00:43:32,079
Speaker 0: - Yeah, nowadays you have your sort of sub stacks and your medium and things like that.

566
00:43:32,300 --> 00:43:34,840
Speaker 1: - Total aside, don't use sub stack, Nazis use it.

567
00:43:35,261 --> 00:43:36,500
Speaker 1: Like seriously, don't use sub stack.

568
00:43:37,440 --> 00:43:56,140
Speaker 0: - Yeah, but I mean, the thing is, as much as sub stack is used by Nazis and is bad and I won't use it, there are some people who write for a living as journalists and it's like, that's basically one of the only ones where they can actually get actual revenues from subscribers that live.

569
00:43:56,860 --> 00:44:03,839
Speaker 0: So I can't really blame them for using it when there isn't another one that does that and also helps build their audience.

570
00:44:03,920 --> 00:44:15,820
Speaker 1: - But that shows the expansion of it's not just access 'cause the idea of the CMS, the content management system to where one person can set up the technology and a different person can write the blog and make the content.

571
00:44:16,460 --> 00:44:27,439
Speaker 1: Now there's other orders of access, monetization, we live in a capitalist world, is one of those things you need to be able to provide to democratize access, to democratize getting ideas out there.

572
00:44:28,600 --> 00:44:35,660
Speaker 0: - So technologically speaking, the earliest blogs were just write some HTML and just you happen to write it in a certain way.

573
00:44:36,042 --> 00:44:47,340
Speaker 0: And then we sort of moved on to the WordPress, well, I guess the Blossom model, which is where you put files in a folder and then a CGI script generates HTML on the fly.

574
00:44:48,046 --> 00:45:13,700
Speaker 0: And then the WordPress model, which is basically still used a lot these days, is basically the same thing only instead of putting text files in a folder on a web server, you go to web form, you fill, you write your post, it's in a database and then people visit the website, the web app queries the database and then server side renders HTML and then sends the HTML to the visitor.

575
00:45:14,324 --> 00:45:15,513
Speaker 0: Or RSS to the web page.

576
00:45:17,080 --> 00:45:22,540
Speaker 0: And now what's interesting today is that a lot of people are actually going backwards.

577
00:45:23,920 --> 00:45:32,300
Speaker 0: One of the most popular things to use these days is not just for blogging, although some of them are, but basically are these static site generators.

578
00:45:33,240 --> 00:45:37,359
Speaker 0: Hugo is an extremely popular one, maybe the most popular one, but there's many of them.

579
00:45:37,920 --> 00:45:41,580
Speaker 1: - I'm actually using Hugo in my microblog.

580
00:45:42,524 --> 00:45:42,799
Speaker 0: - Okay.

581
00:45:43,240 --> 00:45:48,400
Speaker 0: But the way these work is even more primitive than like the old blogs.

582
00:45:48,600 --> 00:46:06,680
Speaker 0: What happens is on your local computer, you write posts in a form, usually markdown, but it could be some other sort of, you know, structured markup language, could be even HTML, but you write posts in some sort of, you know, markup language or just text even.

583
00:46:07,302 --> 00:46:22,440
Speaker 0: And then you push a render button and on your computer, it will look at all your posts, it'll look at the theme and all the templates you have set up and all your configuration files and everything, and it will render the entire website.

584
00:46:22,741 --> 00:46:28,020
Speaker 0: Every page on the website will generate the HTML and all the other CSS, everything.

585
00:46:28,180 --> 00:46:33,280
Speaker 0: It will render every file for the whole website all at once instead of on demand.

586
00:46:33,960 --> 00:46:39,820
Speaker 0: And so then you upload that entire folder, right, to someplace on the internet where it can be delivered.

587
00:46:40,403 --> 00:46:42,500
Speaker 0: And now it's just a static website, right?

588
00:46:42,620 --> 00:46:44,240
Speaker 0: It doesn't change in real time.

589
00:46:44,440 --> 00:46:46,960
Speaker 0: The server doesn't need any CPU to do any rendering.

590
00:46:47,560 --> 00:46:53,100
Speaker 0: You just need to, a user comes to the URL, there's a file at the URL, the server delivers the file.

591
00:46:53,864 --> 00:47:01,899
Speaker 0: If you want to edit your website, you edit it locally on your computer, you push render, and you upload the new version, and now your website's updated.

592
00:47:03,722 --> 00:47:09,520
Speaker 0: And a lot of people are using this method these days because A, it's extremely flexible, right?

593
00:47:09,560 --> 00:47:15,320
Speaker 0: You can customize everything entirely, right, at any part of the pipeline locally.

594
00:47:16,148 --> 00:47:16,638
Speaker 0: It's free.

595
00:47:18,606 --> 00:47:19,660
Speaker 0: That's always a bonus.

596
00:47:21,522 --> 00:47:27,420
Speaker 0: But the hosting becomes way, way cheaper, easier, and faster, right?

597
00:47:28,224 --> 00:47:29,740
Speaker 0: You don't have to pay for a server.

598
00:47:29,901 --> 00:47:35,060
Speaker 0: You can just put your files on, like, GitHub pages or an S3 bucket or something.

599
00:47:35,403 --> 00:47:40,879
Speaker 0: That the hosting is so much cheaper and your website loads so much faster, you don't have to worry about it.

600
00:47:41,181 --> 00:47:42,459
Speaker 0: It's like, oh, no, my server went down.

601
00:47:42,562 --> 00:47:43,198
Speaker 0: That doesn't happen.

602
00:47:43,341 --> 00:47:45,196
Speaker 0: It's just files being delivered at the end.

603
00:47:47,044 --> 00:47:51,160
Speaker 0: And so doing things this old way is like the new popular way of blogging.

604
00:47:51,320 --> 00:47:53,859
Speaker 0: I mean, it even generates the RSS feeds, right?

605
00:47:54,543 --> 00:47:56,017
Speaker 0: Those are just static files also.

606
00:47:57,702 --> 00:48:11,239
Speaker 0: And so if someone was going to want to blog today, that's actually what I would recommend is, you know, Hugo is the most popular one by far, but you could even make your own because it's not a complicated piece of software to write.

607
00:48:12,484 --> 00:48:13,740
Speaker 0: It's a static site generator.

608
00:48:14,441 --> 00:48:22,280
Speaker 0: And then the syndication, right, you have to handle sort of on your own because there's no computation resources, right, going on there.

609
00:48:22,280 --> 00:48:30,380
Speaker 1: Now, that is why, again, this is not like an endorsement or an ad, but that's why I'm using micro.blog for now is that they will handle some of those pieces.

610
00:48:31,321 --> 00:48:42,719
Speaker 1: So they have like a little thing set up that'll automatically, all I have to do is do a credential login or an app password, and they'll cross post to a bunch of different things and handle that for me.

611
00:48:43,062 --> 00:48:45,240
Speaker 1: And I'm paying them a few dollars a month to make that happen.

612
00:48:45,662 --> 00:48:54,460
Speaker 1: But eventually I'll just host that myself, which again ties back to why you got to use your own domain so that you have control over that in case, you know, microblog might go down.

613
00:48:54,660 --> 00:48:56,059
Speaker 1: Cloud plane literally died on me.

614
00:48:57,060 --> 00:48:57,201
Speaker 0: Yep.

615
00:48:57,262 --> 00:49:07,360
Speaker 0: I mean, that's the thing is like, you know, if you have a static website and you put it on, you know, an S3 bucket, it's like you're going to have to use your own domain.

616
00:49:07,529 --> 00:49:07,658
Speaker 1: Yep.

617
00:49:08,320 --> 00:49:18,940
Speaker 0: But if you, let's say you go to, let's say you use Tumblr or Medium or something, it's like, well, you're not, you know, you guess you could pay, I don't know if some of those, like even wordpress.com supports paying and using your own domain.

618
00:49:19,343 --> 00:49:28,491
Speaker 0: But if you don't use your own domain and people start subscribing to you and you want to change where you're hosted or move at all, it's like you lose all that, right?

619
00:49:29,720 --> 00:49:31,539
Speaker 0: Because all your old URLs are just going to die.

620
00:49:32,381 --> 00:49:43,439
Speaker 0: So it's like, no matter what you use, you got to have a custom domain of some kind if you expect to be doing this a long time, to keep it there, to, you know, have followers, be able to follow you.

621
00:49:43,560 --> 00:49:44,300
Speaker 1: I mean, and not for nothing.

622
00:49:44,381 --> 00:49:46,439
Speaker 1: This is something where we have some expertise.

623
00:49:46,700 --> 00:49:53,540
Speaker 1: Geek Nights has been going since 2005 and every fucking thing we've ever made is still on the internet and all our old links still work.

624
00:49:53,820 --> 00:49:57,519
Speaker 1: You know how much of a pain in the ass it was to make all of our old links work forever?

625
00:50:00,323 --> 00:50:01,878
Speaker 0: It's really, you know, it's a pain, yeah.

626
00:50:03,320 --> 00:50:06,940
Speaker 1: Yeah, so micro.blog, they right now just out of the box just click.

627
00:50:07,822 --> 00:50:17,300
Speaker 1: They'll cross post to Medium, Macedon, LinkedIn, I guess if you really want to do that, Tumblr, Flickr, Blue Sky, Noister, PixelFed, and Threads.

628
00:50:18,040 --> 00:50:19,278
Speaker 1: Like, it'll just work.

629
00:50:21,260 --> 00:50:27,500
Speaker 0: Yeah, so yeah, you know, I think blogging, it's like, it's weird that it sort of went out of style.

630
00:50:28,200 --> 00:50:31,780
Speaker 0: I don't know if it's like that much back in style.

631
00:50:31,921 --> 00:50:34,600
Speaker 0: I don't get the feeling that like normal people are.

632
00:50:34,620 --> 00:50:35,920
Speaker 1: Actually, that's something I've been feeling too.

633
00:50:36,020 --> 00:50:51,320
Speaker 1: I feel like, just like you mentioned how Blue Sky is getting like the top 10%, like the invested aggressive users, I feel like blogging and forums are starting to make a comeback, but only among a very certain small group of people globally.

634
00:50:52,222 --> 00:50:57,880
Speaker 0: Yeah, at least, you know, in the English-speaking world, I don't see the rest of the internet because I can't read it very well.

635
00:50:59,141 --> 00:51:17,880
Speaker 0: But yeah, it feels like people who are, you know, deeply involved in something, right, are the very engaged people, are the kind that will find a blog, right, which is also a deeply engaged person who's writing deeply, usually on just a single topic or a handful of topics.

636
00:51:17,980 --> 00:51:29,516
Speaker 1: Well, here, what's better, going to a Reddit thread or following a user on Reddit who gives ski reviews or finding some guy out there who is an expert on skis and subscribing to his crap wherever it is?

637
00:51:32,144 --> 00:51:35,119
Speaker 0: But yeah, it's like, so, I don't know.

638
00:51:35,740 --> 00:51:36,552
Speaker 1: Yeah, well, I guess...

639
00:51:37,080 --> 00:51:43,960
Speaker 0: It'll be interesting to see what happens going forward, right, as different social medias or, you know, build up.

640
00:51:44,101 --> 00:51:46,140
Speaker 0: Will they sap the energy again?

641
00:51:46,320 --> 00:51:47,859
Speaker 0: Will they help to amplify blogging?

642
00:51:47,900 --> 00:51:53,060
Speaker 1: What I think, and Blue Sky made me think about this a lot because I'm not, like, Blue Sky is going to die.

643
00:51:53,160 --> 00:51:53,577
Speaker 1: They all die.

644
00:51:53,820 --> 00:52:16,240
Speaker 1: But the fact that so many people migrated so quickly to it when all it was is what Twitter used to be a long time ago, I think there is this deep hunger in society for the content and easy ways to interact with it free from the baleful gaze of advertising, capitalism, brands, and bullshit.

645
00:52:17,781 --> 00:52:37,859
Speaker 1: And there is always a subset of people who will use the technology to make that exist in the world, and that's what's happening again, where we always, now that the Internet exists, people like us keep fleeing to the places where capitalism isn't ruining everything as best we can because the content is what people want.

646
00:52:38,542 --> 00:52:43,540
Speaker 1: But I think the other thing that's happening is that the technology behind it barely matters because it's kind of a hockey stick.

647
00:52:43,780 --> 00:52:47,200
Speaker 1: I mean, the Internet was invented not that long ago.

648
00:52:47,600 --> 00:52:49,699
Speaker 1: Blogging appeared not that long ago.

649
00:52:50,120 --> 00:52:52,019
Speaker 1: Social media is brand new in the world.

650
00:52:52,601 --> 00:53:01,540
Speaker 1: I feel like everything that's happened in the last 20 years of the Internet is going to be in the history books of, like, how humans communicate with each other.

651
00:53:02,042 --> 00:53:05,200
Speaker 1: It's like one sentence compared to what's coming.

652
00:53:05,803 --> 00:53:22,680
Speaker 1: So the content itself, the actual creative or intellectual or whatever it is, the pornography, everything, the content itself, that is all that matters, and the way we get it to each other is going to evolve so rapidly that you can't invest in any particular technology or platform.

653
00:53:23,081 --> 00:53:30,100
Speaker 1: You've just got to focus on making sure the content's available and adapting to how the technology makes that possible.

654
